"Fred M. Sloniker" <fizzit@pioneernet.net> writes:
> There's an idea I've had for a change to NetHack for a while, and I'm
> curious how difficult it'd be to implement (ranging, presumably, from
> 'here, lemme knock those five lines of code out for you' to 'perhaps you'd
> prefer to write your own roguelike').  Specifically, I'm interested in
> changing the behavior of a wish for a unique item that already exists so
> that, at least in some cases, the wish succeeds.
> 
> To the nitty gritty: suppose you're down on DL 15 or something and you get a
> wish, so you wish for Magicbane.  Unbeknownst to you, some kobold on level
> 1 found it, picked it up, and happened not to encounter your wrath before
> you left.  In standard Nethack, your wish would give you that hyperdrive-
> motivator-has-been-damaged feeling.  I'd like to suggest that you get it.

The main problem with this idea is purely technical; the game knows
that Magicbane already exists or has existed (its entry in the
artiexist[] array has been set true), but it doesn't have any direct
knowledge of _where_ it is. To find it, it would need to:

* look through your inventory (you might be forgetful)
* look through the object chain of the current level
* look through the monster chain of the current level and check the
  inventory of each
* look through the migrating object chain
* look through the migrating monster chain and check the inventory
  of each
* load in each of the stored levelfiles and look through the object chains
  and the monster inventories of each of these

and, if this doesn't turn up Magicbane, conclude that it has existed
but has been destroyed.

I don't even know how possible the last step of that is; certainly
it's not something the code does as presently structured, only loading
a level if you're visiting it. Then you'd need to _change_ that
levelfile if it's the one with Magicbane on, and resave it... brrr.

